    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4; Section 1.1.1, equation (1.10)",
      "anchor": "for any two points x₁ and x₂",
      "observation": "The integral conservation law is d/dt ∫[x₁,x₂] q(x,t) dx = f(q(x₁,t)) − f(q(x₂,t))."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4; paragraph immediately following equation (1.10)",
      "anchor": "Each component of q measures the density of some conserved quantity",
      "observation": "The integral measures the total amount of the conserved quantity between the endpoints; this amount changes only through endpoint fluxes. Nonconservation, such as chemical reactions, requires source terms."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF pages 26–27; printed pages 4–5; Section 1.1.2",
      "anchor": "provided that q and f(q) are sufficiently smooth; integral conservation law (1.10) ... does continue to hold",
      "observation": "Derivation of the differential equation from the integral equation requires sufficient smoothness. At a discontinuity the differential equation fails in the classical sense, whereas the integral conservation law continues to hold."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; Section 1.2, first paragraph",
      "anchor": "break the domain into grid cells",
      "observation": "Finite volume methods use the integral conservation law and approximate the integral over each cell, or the cell average obtained by dividing that integral by cell volume."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; Section 1.2, first paragraph",
      "anchor": "These values are modified in each time step by the flux through the edges of the grid cells",
      "observation": "The time evolution of the cell quantities is determined through fluxes across cell edges."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; Section 1.2, first paragraph",
      "anchor": "numerical flux functions ... based on the approximate cell averages, the only data available",
      "observation": "Numerical flux functions should approximate the correct fluxes reasonably well using the approximate cell averages."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; Section 1.2.1, equation (1.11) and following paragraph",
      "anchor": "q_l = Q_{i−1} and q_r = Q_i",
      "observation": "As surrounding methodological context, a Riemann problem with neighboring cell averages as its left and right states supplies information that can be used to compute a numerical flux and update cell averages over a time step."
    }
  ],
  "statement": {
    "binders": [
      "q(x,t) denotes the state as a function of spatial position x and time t; its components represent densities of conserved quantities. The component count is not specified in this passage. [Printed p. 4, opening paragraph and Section 1.1.1, explanation after (1.10).]",
      "f is the physical flux function evaluated on q. The surrounding discussion allows nonlinear flux functions and mentions the linear example f(q)=Aq. [Printed p. 4, opening paragraph and (1.10).]",
      "x₁ and x₂ are arbitrary spatial endpoints: the source explicitly says 'for any two points'. No explicit time interval or endpoint-order restriction is supplied. [Printed p. 4, Section 1.1.1, introduction to (1.10).]",
      "Grid cells and successive time steps are the numerical objects in the selected paragraph; no cell-index set, mesh parameters, or quantified time-step range is introduced there. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph.]"
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      "The conservation setting has changes in the total amount accounted for only by boundary fluxes; processes that do not conserve the quantity require source terms beyond the displayed balance. [Printed p. 4, paragraph after (1.10).]",
      "The domain is divided into grid cells, and the numerical data consist of approximate cell averages. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph, 'break the domain into grid cells' and 'the only data available'.]",
      "Sufficient smoothness of q and f(q) is a condition for deriving the classical differential equation from the integral equation; it is not presented as a condition restricting finite volume methods to smooth solutions. [Printed pp. 4–5, Section 1.1.2; printed p. 5, Section 1.2, opening sentences.]"
    ],
    "conclusions": [
      "The continuum balance is d/dt ∫[x₁,x₂] q(x,t) dx = f(q(x₁,t)) − f(q(x₂,t)): the first endpoint flux enters with a plus sign and the second with a minus sign. [Printed p. 4, equation (1.10).]",
      "Finite volume methods approximate cell integrals or cell averages and modify these quantities at each time step through cell-edge fluxes. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph.]",
      "The numerical task is to construct flux functions that approximate the correct fluxes reasonably well from the available approximate cell averages. This is a methodological requirement, without a quantitative error conclusion in the passage. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph, sentence beginning 'These values are modified'.]",
      "The integral conservation formulation continues to apply at discontinuities, where the differential equation does not hold in the classical sense. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.1.2, paragraph beginning 'At a discontinuity'.]"
    ],
    "definitions_and_conventions": [
      "A cell average is the integral of q over the cell divided by the volume of that cell. The integral and average therefore have distinct normalization. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph.]",
      "The phrase 'total mass' refers to the total amount of the conserved quantity represented by a component of q, rather than exclusively physical mass. [Printed p. 4, explanation immediately after (1.10).]",
      "Physical flux is denoted f(q); numerical flux functions approximate the correct fluxes using approximate cell averages. No formula identifying these two types of flux is given. [Printed p. 4, (1.10); printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph.]"
    ],
    "implicit_context": [
      "The displayed inherited conservation equation uses one spatial coordinate and two endpoints; the numerical description uses the terms cell volume and cell edges without specifying a mesh geometry. [Printed p. 4, (1.10); printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph.]",
      "Discontinuous solutions, including shock waves, motivate the use of the integral formulation; discontinuities may develop from smooth initial data. [Printed pp. 4–5, Section 1.1.2.]",
      "The following subsection provides a possible construction using neighboring cell averages Q_{i−1} and Q_i as Riemann initial states. It says the resulting information can be used for a numerical flux, without making that construction a universal requirement on every method described in Section 1.2. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2.1, paragraph after (1.11).]"
    ],
    "semantic_facets": [
      {
        "topic": "Conservation and orientation",
        "details": [
          "The inherited exact balance is a signed difference of physical endpoint fluxes, with signs fixed by equation (1.10). [Printed p. 4, (1.10).]",
          "Cell quantities change through their boundary fluxes; the selected numerical paragraph does not display a discrete conservation identity or a global telescoping sum. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph.]"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Approximation and available data",
        "details": [
          "The numerical state approximates integrals or averages rather than point values at grid points. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph, 'Rather than pointwise approximations'.]",
          "Numerical fluxes are based on approximate cell averages; the passage specifies no stencil width or exclusive dependence on two adjacent states. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph, 'based on the approximate cell averages'.]"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Temporal and geometric specification",
        "details": [
          "Updates occur in each time step, but the selected passage supplies no temporal quadrature, explicit recurrence, time-step coefficient, or cell-width symbol. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph.]",
          "Cell-volume normalization is explicit in the average definition; uniform cell size is not stipulated. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph, definition of cell average.]"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "undebatable_constraints": [
    "Preserve the distinction between a cell integral and its volume-normalized average. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, definition of cell average.]",
    "Preserve the order and signs of the physical endpoint fluxes in equation (1.10). [Printed p. 4, (1.10).]",
    "Preserve the use of approximate cell averages as data for numerical flux construction. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, 'the only data available'.]",
