  "contract_plain_english": "Finite volume methods divide the domain into cells and approximate the amount of a conserved quantity in each cell, usually expressed as its cell average: the integral divided by cell volume. Each time step changes these quantities through cell-edge fluxes, with numerical flux functions constructed from the available approximate cell averages to approximate the correct fluxes reasonably well. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph.] The inherited continuum balance identifies the rate of change of the integral between two endpoints with the first endpoint's physical flux minus the second endpoint's physical flux. [Printed p. 4, equation (1.10) and its explanation.] The integral formulation remains applicable at discontinuities, unlike the classical differential equation. [Printed pp. 4–5, Section 1.1.2.] The selected passage does not uniquely specify a discrete recurrence, flux normalization, stencil, or quantitative accuracy guarantee. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph.]"
